  <h2 id="revision">One-minute revision</h2>
  <Callout kind="revision">
    <ul>
      <li>L2 norm <Math tex="= \sqrt{\sum a_i^2} = \sqrt{\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{a}}" />. L1 <Math tex="= \sum|a_i|" />. L∞ <Math tex="= \max|a_i|" />. For (3, 4): 5, 7, 4.</li>
      <li>Unit balls: L1 diamond (corners → sparsity), L2 circle (smooth shrinkage), L∞ square.</li>
      <li>Distance = norm of the difference. <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{a}-\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 = \lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert^2 + \lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert^2 - 2\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}" />.</li>
      <li>Cosine <Math tex="= \mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}/(\lVert\mathbf{a}\rVert\lVert\mathbf{b}\rVert)" />, in [−1, 1], ignores length.</li>
      <li>Dot rewards length; cosine ignores it; Euclidean measures position. Text embeddings: cosine.</li>
      <li>Normalize to unit length and all three rankings agree; dot = cosine; distance² = 2 − 2cos.</li>
      <li>“Normalize” means three things: per vector (L2), per feature column (standardize), per activation (LayerNorm/RMSNorm). Ask which axis.</li>
      <li>RMSNorm divides by <Math tex="\lVert\mathbf{x}\rVert/\sqrt{d}" />. Gradient clipping scales all gradients so their joint L2 norm ≤ threshold.</li>
      <li>High dimensions: random cosines ≈ 0 ± <Math tex="1/\sqrt{d}" />; distances concentrate; thresholds are model-specific.</li>
    </ul>
  </Callout>
</Note>
